\section{Reporting failures where a human can find them}
\label{sec:diagnostics}

Raising [[GitError]] only moves the problem: the callers in
[[__init__.py]] must keep swallowing everything, because transparency
means a student's program may not crash, and may not acquire extra
output, just because learnlog had a bad day.
What transparency does \emph{not} require is that the failure leave no
trace at all.
Today a dropped run and a clean run look identical from every angle---to
the student, to the teacher reading the log, and to us reading a bug
report---and that is what makes these failures so expensive to diagnose.

So the exception handlers keep swallowing, and [[report_error]] gives
the evidence somewhere to land.
Two channels, because two different people need it at two different
times.
[[.learnlog/errors.log]] is written unconditionally and read later: it
is the record that explains a gap in a student's history weeks after the
fact, and it costs the running program one append.
[[LEARNLOG_DEBUG]] additionally mirrors each entry to standard error,
for whoever is debugging learnlog \emph{now} and should not have to know
that the file exists.

<<constants>>=
ERRORS_LOG_NAME = "errors.log"
DEBUG_ENV_VAR = "LEARNLOG_DEBUG"
<<functions>>=
def report_error(context, error, workdir=None):
    """Record a learnlog failure without disturbing the program.

    Appends a timestamped entry to ``.learnlog/errors.log`` under
    ``workdir`` (the current directory by default) and, when
    ``LEARNLOG_DEBUG`` is set in the environment, mirrors it to the
    real standard error.  Never raises.
    """
    timestamp = datetime.datetime.now().isoformat()
    entry = (
        f"{timestamp} {context}: "
        f"{type(error).__name__}: {error}"
    )
    <<mirror the entry to stderr when debugging>>
    <<append the entry to the errors log>>
@

The mirror writes to [[sys.__stderr__]], not [[sys.stderr]]: by the time
anything can fail, learnlog has usually replaced [[sys.stderr]] with its
own capturing wrapper, and a diagnostic sent through that wrapper would
be recorded in the commit as though the student's program had printed
it.
[[sys.__stderr__]] is [[None]] in interpreters started without a console
(pythonw, some embedded hosts), so we fall back to whatever
[[sys.stderr]] currently is.

<<mirror the entry to stderr when debugging>>=
if os.environ.get(DEBUG_ENV_VAR):
    stream = sys.__stderr__ or sys.stderr
    try:
        print(f"learnlog: {entry}", file=stream)
    except Exception:
        pass
@

We append only to a [[.learnlog/]] that already exists.
The alternative---creating it---would mean that a learnlog failure in a
directory that is not a learnlog project silently starts one, which is
precisely the surprise the diagnostics are supposed to prevent.

Writing the log \emph{inside} the Git directory is what keeps it out of
the history it describes: [[.learnlog]] is the first exclude pattern
(\cref{sec:init-repo}), so [[git add .]] never stages anything under it.
Git's built-in protection does not apply here---that rule is hard-coded
for a directory literally named [[.git]]---which is why the exclude
pattern and this path are derived from the same constant.

Finally, every failure inside [[report_error]] is itself swallowed---
including the path handling, not just the write.
This is the one place where that matters more than it looks: the only
callers are [[except]] blocks whose whole purpose is to stop an
exception from reaching the student's program, so an exception escaping
from here would do precisely the damage those handlers exist to
prevent.
A read-only checkout, a full disk, or a nonsensical [[workdir]] must not
turn a reported failure into an unreported crash.

<<append the entry to the errors log>>=
try:
    git_dir = (
        pathlib.Path(workdir or os.getcwd()) / GIT_DIR_NAME
    )
    if not git_dir.is_dir():
        return
    with open(
        git_dir / ERRORS_LOG_NAME, "a", encoding="utf-8"
    ) as log:
        log.write(entry + "\n")
except Exception:
    pass
@

Let's verify both channels, and the exclusion that keeps the log out of
the history:

<<test functions>>=
def test_report_error_appends_to_log(workdir):
    repo = LearnlogRepo(workdir)
    report_error("begin_run", ValueError("boom"), workdir)
    log = (workdir / ".learnlog" / "errors.log").read_text()
    assert "begin_run" in log
    assert "ValueError: boom" in log

def test_report_error_without_repo_is_silent(tmp_path):
    report_error("begin_run", ValueError("boom"), tmp_path)
    assert not (tmp_path / ".learnlog").exists()

def test_report_error_never_raises(tmp_path):
    """An unusable target must not become a second failure."""
    report_error("begin_run", ValueError("boom"), "\0bad")

def test_report_error_debug_writes_stderr(
    workdir, monkeypatch, capsys
):
    LearnlogRepo(workdir)
    monkeypatch.setenv("LEARNLOG_DEBUG", "1")
    monkeypatch.setattr(sys, "__stderr__", sys.stderr)
    report_error("on_exit", ValueError("boom"), workdir)
    assert "ValueError: boom" in capsys.readouterr().err

def test_report_error_quiet_without_debug(
    workdir, monkeypatch, capsys
):
    LearnlogRepo(workdir)
    monkeypatch.delenv("LEARNLOG_DEBUG", raising=False)
    monkeypatch.setattr(sys, "__stderr__", sys.stderr)
    report_error("on_exit", ValueError("boom"), workdir)
    assert capsys.readouterr().err == ""

def test_errors_log_is_never_staged(workdir):
    repo = LearnlogRepo(workdir)
    report_error("begin_run", ValueError("boom"), workdir)
    repo.git("add", ".")
    assert "errors.log" not in repo.git("ls-files").stdout
